 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 241447,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A plant has a measured pressure potential Ψp = 0.21MPa and a solute potential Ψs =-3.50MPa. The soil is saturated with water because it rained.  After three months of dry weather, the soil has dried out. Make a claim to answer the following: How will the water move? How will the water potential of the soil compare to the water potential measured immediately before the rain? How will the stomata respond to the change in weather?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 755250,
     "content_html": "The water will move from the plant to the soil. Dry soil has a lower water potential than wet soil. Under drought conditions, the stomata close to conserve water and leaves may also be shed if the drought continues."
    },
    {
     "id": 755249,
     "content_html": "The water will move from the soil to the plant. Dry soil has a higher water potential than wet soil. Under drought conditions, the stomata close to conserve water and leaves may also be shed if the drought continues."
    },
    {
     "id": 755248,
     "content_html": "The water will move from the soil to the plant. Dry soil has a lower water potential than wet soil. Under drought conditions, the stomata open pores wider in order to perform a better rate of transpiration."
    },
    {
     "id": 755251,
     "content_html": "The water will move from the soil to the plant. Dry soil has a lower water potential than wet soil. Under drought conditions, the stomata close to conserve water and leaves may also be shed if the drought continues."
    }
   ],
